\title{INT8: the three changes of the optimisation wave together - the gates and the guard of the merged build, and where it stands against gruszka}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{performance}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{arena}
\tag{platform}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Integration of the optimisation wave of the night of 2026-11-24 to 2026-11-25 (the windows of this report: 09:19 to 10:39 UTC on 2026-11-25). Three changes had been measured one by one, each against a base of its own and mostly on a busy host, and were then applied to the main tree and published together: [the hand-kept inline list of the image](log-E7BA) ([rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A)), [the level base that the workers share](log-ZMAE) ([rep-OPT-C](rep-OPT-C)) and [the loops without a call in the reader, the loader and the block writer](log-R7NE) ([rep-OPT-B](rep-OPT-B)). This report is the verification of the build that has all three: the gates that had not been run on it, the guard of [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N) against the release build before the wave, on a quiet host, with the arena checker gruszka in the same windows, and the rows of [the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks). No source was changed by this work. Oracle GraalVM was used for one thing only, the optional profile-guided build of section 4.1. The decision that rests on this report is [dec-JT59](dec-JT59).}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The gates pass on the merged build, with the counts of the release} (section 2): the gate of the default configuration through the jars, as it had through the executable; the gate of the reference engine by name; the strict policy; the eight corpora with 8 and 4 workers on both launchers and under the strict policy with no decline; the driver's invariance suite; the machine's fast group and its slow hostile matrix; and the arena's own procedure on its 270 tests through two executables. No wrong accept and no wrong reject anywhere. Every counter of a deterministic run is identical to the release's on the eight corpora and both launchers: the three changes together are cost only.}
\li{\strong{The guard holds on both launchers} (section 3), on a quiet host, three interleaved rounds per corpus: in the executable the geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes from 2.030 to 1.853 s, -8.7 \%, and every corpus is faster (3.2 to 10.7 \%); on the JVM launcher from 1.603 to 1.553 s, -3.2 \%, with reachability at +1.4 \% as the largest loss. Mathlib: the executable 14.57 to 13.11 s with a peak resident set of 4.3 GiB where it was 7.4 GiB, the JVM launcher 11.68 to 11.00 s. The three gains add up in the executable: the product of the three single measurements is 0.919, together they give 0.913.}
\li{\strong{By the arena's metric} the merged executable needs 1,462.3 G instructions on Mathlib where the release needed 1,652.6 G, 11.5 \% fewer, and 7 to 13 \% fewer on every corpus (section 2.3).}
\li{\strong{Against gruszka, plainly} (sections 3.3 and 4), 8 workers on 8 cores of a quiet host, gruszka with its arena run line in the same windows: the merged executable needs 1.22 times gruszka's wall clock on Mathlib (13.11 against 10.75 s), 1.32 times on navier-stokes-euler and 1.18 times on cslib, where the release needed 1.35, 1.47 and 1.29 times. Over all eight corpora it is behind gruszka on seven, by 6 to 31 \% for a warm pass, and ahead on reachability. The JVM launcher, a development tool, is level with gruszka on Mathlib and cslib (1.02 and 0.98) and 16 \% behind on navier-stokes-euler. The wave closed a third of the executable's distance on the three large corpora; two thirds are left.}
\li{\strong{The benchmark tables changed their condition with this report}: their rows are now taken with the other cores of the host idle and with the baselines in the same windows. Under that condition gruszka is 10 to 32 \% faster than the earlier tables showed and RLVM 4 to 14 \%, so the ratios to gruszka of the new tables are less favourable than those of the old ones although the executable got faster on every corpus (section 4).}
\li{\strong{The optional build with Oracle GraalVM} was given a new profile and built once: Mathlib 12.93 s cold and 12.41 s warm. It now needs 0.92 to 0.96 times the default executable's wall clock, where it needed 0.85 to 0.89: the inline list gave the default executable a part of what the profile gives (section 4.1).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was verified, against what}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The merged build.} The shared distribution built on 2026-11-25 at 09:12 UTC by \src{scripts/build.sh} from the published jars (jars sha256 \code{30dadd7f6cc5}), development layout: the executable \code{rlvm} (GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3 native image, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~}, \code{\verb~|-R:MaxHeapSize=13g~}, \code{\verb~|-R:InitialCollectionPolicy=BySpaceAndTime~}, the inline lists with 269 forced and 81 never-inlined methods; 68,356,216 bytes, sha256 \code{3b4dfc34d447}) and the JVM launcher \code{rlvm-jvm} on the same jars (HotSpot of GraalVM CE 25 with C2, serial collector, heap of the machine \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}). Jars that the wave changed: rlvm-level \code{4db74adbb68d}, rlvm-api \code{e2d2d31509fc}, rlvm-export \code{38c922c4fccd}, rlvm-store \code{a93a52584ee7}, rlvm-load \code{a2a091b291fa}. Unchanged: rlvm-machine \code{fa6d5d3bf377}, rlvm-driver \code{31fa08d9ce61}, rlvm-ref \code{a20c24a26b7c}, rlvm-main \code{3729609561e3}, rlvm-admit \code{398a117d4ad8}, rlvm-inductive \code{c15b14fffdd3}, rlvm-nat \code{b1dda7562adb}, rlvm-cli \code{cd4cbbee7ad1}, rlvm-truffle \code{ad3256d0751d}. The three runs of \src{scripts/gate.sh} ran on that distribution; the runs of the corpora, the counters, the guard and the arena's procedure ran on a byte-identical copy of it; the invariance suite and the machine's tests ran the published jars themselves.}
\li{\strong{The baseline.} The release build before the wave: the shared distribution of 03:18 UTC (jars sha256 \code{162de4229b52}; the executable sha256 \code{c3185afb415d}, 194 forced and 81 never-inlined methods), in a byte-identical copy that was kept from that hour. It is the build of [the profile of the release](rep-P11), of [the arena's procedure on the final build](rep-AR2) and of the benchmark tables before this report.}
\li{\strong{A release-layout executable} of the merged jars, built for the arena's procedure by \code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --published~} with the third-party jars checked against \src{scripts/arena/deps.sha256}: one file, sha256 \code{b8c74c893326}, the same size as the development executable and not byte-identical to it, as with every second build of a native image; its \code{\verb~|--version~} output is the development executable's.}
\li{\strong{Configuration.} A run without \code{\verb~|-X engine~}: the machine with its default switch set (\code{\verb~|gated,pchk,expect,warm,facts,code,casetrees,probes,bodies,directrules,same,match,joint,lazycases,lazyeval,eager,skipargs,congruence~}), policy practical, load pipelined with checking. The reference engine is named where it is meant.}
\li{\strong{Already established by the publication} of the three changes, on the same shared distribution, and not repeated here: the suites of the five changed modules from the tested snapshots their jars were built from; the gate of the default configuration through the executable (\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile --native~}): 230 of 230 published tests, 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 (agree 15,741, declined 2,034), 1,196 hostile exports (agree 1,124, 68 tolerated accepts, 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 4 declined); the arena definition built and run end to end from a clean copy.}
\li{\strong{Host.} aarch64, 10 Cortex-X925 and 10 Cortex-A725, 121 GB. Gates and other runs that are not timings ran behind \src{bench/slot.sh}; every time in seconds is from a \src{bench/quiet.py} window on the 8 measurement cores.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The gates of the merged build}

\p{Every oracle below is an official verdict: the arena's expected outcome of a published test, the stored verdict of the official checker for a mutant, the manifest's column for a hostile export. All gates ran on the build of section 1, between 09:19 and 09:45 UTC; no gate had to be run twice.}

