\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|SHostileSlowTest~}}\td{main tree, against the published jars}\td{2 tests, 0 failures: 56 switch sets by 1,196 hostile exports, 0 unexpected under every set}}
\tr{\td{The arena's own procedure, 270 tests}\td{\code{rlvm}, the run line of the arena definition; development and release-layout executable}\td{no wrong accept, no wrong reject, no error, no timeout on both executables: good 148 / 148 accepted, bad 92 / 92 rejected, either 11 accepted / 17 rejected / 2 declined; Mathlib 1,462.3 G instructions (section 2.3)}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No timeout this time.} The gates of the three changes had each met two mutants of \code{bugs/proj-of-subst-prop} and \code{bugs/rec-of-subst-prop} with a changed literal at the judge's limit of 60 s on a loaded host. Here the gate through the jars ran alone on the efficiency cores, the reference engine's gate on the performance cores, and the corpus \code{bugs} ended with agree 2,590 and declined 102 in the first run of both.}
\li{\strong{The counts are those of the release} in every row: no count of [the gate](design-gate) moved.}
\li{\strong{The invariance suite} runs every file under 9 schedules: batch with 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers, a session cap of one byte, and pipelined with a publication after every unit, every 7 and every 64 units. It is the gate of the shared level base: a worker's overlay is moved to a new snapshot at every publication.}
}

