\ul{
\li{\strong{Mathlib: 1,462.3 G instructions}, 243.7 virtual seconds of the arena, 11.5 percent below the release's 1,652.6 G. The release-layout executable: 1,462.6 G, 13.65 s. Four single runs, two per executable: 1,462.02 to 1,462.84 G, 13.55 to 13.90 s of wall clock, 101.4 s of CPU, peak RSS 3,658 to 3,720 MiB (the release: 4,757 to 5,614 MiB).}
\li{\strong{Every corpus needs 7 to 13 percent fewer instructions}; the 262 small tests together 595.3 G against 638.1 G.}
\li{\strong{By the arena's sort key} nothing changes but the Mathlib count: no bad test accepted, no good test rejected, 2 declined. The leaders on this host, with their arena run lines and 4 threads (rpylean with 1), as quoted by rep-AR2 and not measured again: nano-lean 361.2 G, gruszka 419.1 G, sokonanoda 505.6 G, rpylean 544.4 G, pestka 1,339.6 G. RLVM needs 4.05, 3.49, 2.89, 2.69 and 1.09 times their instructions. RLVM has never been measured on x86-64, the arena's architecture. Scaled as rep-AR2 scaled its count, by the ratios between the leaders' published counts and their counts here (0.95 to 1.11), the 1,462.3 G become 1,393 to 1,625 G: pestka's published 1,475.3 G lies inside that range, so the projection is place 5 or place 6, and place 5 only if RLVM's count on x86-64 is at most 1.009 times its count here; pestka itself counts 1.10 times as much there as here.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The guard of the merged build against the release}

\p{[The rule for a default](dec-YP0N): on the eight corpora the geometric mean of the warm passes goes down and no corpus is more than 3 \% slower, over at least three interleaved rounds. Warm pass that reloads the file, 8 workers, load pipelined with checking, a run without options but \code{\verb~|--threads 8 --stats -X passes=N~}; the JVM launcher with its own heap line for the machine, \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}. One quiet window per corpus on the 8 measurement cores, with four variants alternating round by round inside it: the executable before, the executable merged, the JVM launcher before, the JVM launcher merged, then the same in reverse order, then as in the first round. A process makes 15 passes on reachability, 10 on Init and Std, 8 on cedar, 6 on con-leche, cslib and navier-stokes-euler and 4 on Mathlib. A cell is the mean over the three rounds of the median later pass of one process, in brackets the smallest and the largest of the rounds; on a pass shorter than 0.5 s it is the mean of the later passes. Peak RSS is the largest value that a pass line of the three rounds reports. "Before" is the release build before the wave, "after" the merged build (section 1). All 96 processes ended with status 0 and ACCEPT in every pass.}

\p{\strong{The host was quiet}: no other job ran during these windows, 09:45 to 10:20 UTC. Before each window fewer than 0.15 CPUs of the whole host were busy over five seconds, and during the windows 0.07 to 0.17 CPUs outside the 8 measurement cores (section 3.4). This is the condition of [the profile of the release](rep-P11) and not that of the benchmark tables before this report, which were taken while the other 12 cores were busy.}

