}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The guard is met}: the geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes from 2.030 to 1.853 s, -8.7 \%, and every corpus is faster, from -3.2 \% (reachability) to -10.7 \% (cedar). No round of the merged build is as slow as the fastest round of the release on any corpus.}
\li{\strong{Mathlib} 14.57 to 13.11 s, -10.0 \%, 1.45 s. The rounds: 14.77, 14.44 and 14.49 s before, 13.18, 13.08 and 13.08 s after. The CPU time of a pass goes from 110.4 to 101.5 s, -8.1 \%: workers 97.1 to 90.0 s, loader thread 11.4 to 9.9 s.}
\li{\strong{The three gains add up.} Measured one by one, each against its own base, the three changes took the geometric mean to 0.966 (the list), 0.959 (the loader's loops) and 0.992 (the level base, quiet host) of the base's, a product of 0.919; together they give 0.913. On Mathlib 0.956, 0.974 and 0.973, a product of 0.906; together 0.900. They act on different parts of a pass: the list on the workers' code, the loops on the loader thread, the shared base on the workers' page faults and the collector.}
\li{\strong{Each change is visible in its own column.} The workers' CPU is 3 to 8 \% lower on every corpus (Mathlib 97.1 to 90.0 s, navier-stokes-euler 24.4 to 22.7 s): the list, and on Mathlib and cslib the copies. The loader thread's CPU is 13 to 21 \% lower on the seven corpora that have a load to speak of (Mathlib 11.39 to 9.88 s, cslib 4.59 to 4.00 s, Init 0.60 to 0.49 s): the loops. The workers' page faults fall from 1.52 to 0.54 million per Mathlib pass and from 0.49 to 0.29 million on cslib, their kernel time from 1.53 to 0.67 s and from 0.53 to 0.32 s, the collections of a Mathlib pass from 34 to 17 and its pauses from 274 to 142 ms: the shared base.}
\li{\strong{Peak resident set}: Mathlib 7,585 to 4,425 MiB, cslib 2,481 to 1,993, navier-stokes-euler 1,656 to 1,481, cedar 875 to 767, Std 676 to 607, Init 409 to 386; reachability and con-leche as before (740 and 738, 1,363 and 1,348).}
}
}

