\ul{
\li{\strong{The guard is met}: the geometric mean goes from 1.603 to 1.553 s, -3.2 \%; seven corpora are 1.4 to 5.8 \% faster and reachability is at +1.4 \%, inside the limit of 3 \%.}
\li{\strong{reachability}, +1.4 \% by the statistic of the rule for a pass under 0.5 s, the mean of the later passes: the rounds are 325, 332 and 339 ms before and 341, 336 and 333 ms after, and the medians of the later passes are 0.3170 and 0.3172 s. Its load is 30 ms of the pass and its level copies were 2 MB: neither change has anything to move there, and the workers' CPU is 0.74 and 0.73 s. A difference of 5 ms between two sets of three rounds that overlap is not a cost.}
\li{\strong{Mathlib} 11.68 to 11.00 s, -5.8 \%. The rounds of the release spread more than those of the merged build: 11.25, 12.08 and 11.71 s against 11.02, 10.94 and 11.04 s. Loader thread 8.91 to 7.58 s of CPU, pauses 259 to 142 ms per pass, collections 10 to 5, peak resident set 6,344 to 5,065 MiB.}
\li{\strong{Less than the two single measurements suggested.} One by one the loader's loops had given 0.962 and the level base 0.981 on the JVM launcher, a product of 0.944; together, here, 0.968. Those two guards were taken while the other cores of the host were busy, this one with them idle, and the JVM's rounds spread by up to 7 \% (Mathlib, the release). The loader thread's CPU is down by 11 to 17 \% on the seven corpora that have a load. How much of the difference is the condition and how much is overlap was not separated: the single changes were not measured again.}
\li{\strong{The workers' CPU} is level on six corpora (Init 2.94 and 2.99 s, navier-stokes-euler 19.30 and 19.31 s) and lower on cslib and Mathlib (76.6 to 75.3 s): on the JVM the workers only lose the copies.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.3 Against gruszka, in the same windows}

\p{gruszka is the arena checker as built for the baseline tables (version \code{ebc80dc}; nothing was rebuilt), run in the arena container on the same 8 cores under \src{bench/measure.sh} with its arena run line and 8 workers (\code{\verb~|scripts/arena-run FILE 8~}), as in rep-P11, section 5.1. In each of the three windows it ran before every round and after the last one, two runs each time: eight runs per corpus, between RLVM's processes. Its wall clock is that of one process from start to exit; RLVM's is a warm pass; one cold process of the merged executable is 3 to 5 \% longer than its warm pass on these three corpora (section 4).}

