\tr{\td{the executable before the wave / gruszka: wall, CPU}\td{1.35, 1.73}\td{1.47, 1.86}\td{1.29, 1.71}}
\tr{\td{\strong{the merged executable / gruszka}: wall, CPU}\td{\strong{1.22}, 1.59}\td{\strong{1.32}, 1.71}\td{\strong{1.18}, 1.59}}
\tr{\td{the JVM launcher before the wave / gruszka: wall, CPU}\td{1.09, 1.38}\td{1.18, 1.49}\td{1.03, 1.36}}
\tr{\td{\strong{the merged JVM launcher / gruszka}: wall, CPU}\td{\strong{1.02}, 1.33}\td{\strong{1.16}, 1.48}\td{\strong{0.98}, 1.32}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The executable is 18 to 32 \% behind gruszka} on these three corpora on a quiet host: Mathlib 13.11 against 10.75 s, navier-stokes-euler 3.49 against 2.64 s, cslib 4.82 against 4.08 s. Before the wave it was 29 to 47 \% behind (1.35, 1.47 and 1.29 times gruszka's wall clock; rep-P11 had 1.34, 1.45 and 1.29 for the same build). The wave closed a third of the distance on each: the surplus over gruszka's wall clock went from 35 to 22 \% on Mathlib, from 47 to 32 \% on navier-stokes-euler and from 29 to 18 \% on cslib.}
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher is level with gruszka on Mathlib and cslib}, 11.00 against 10.75 s (1.02) and 4.01 against 4.08 s (0.98), and 16 \% behind on navier-stokes-euler (3.06 against 2.64 s). It is a development tool and not what a release ships; the executable is what counts.}
\li{\strong{In CPU the distance is larger}: the executable needs 1.59, 1.71 and 1.59 times gruszka's CPU time (before: 1.73, 1.86, 1.71), the JVM launcher 1.33, 1.48 and 1.32 times. RLVM stays closer in wall clock than in CPU because it checks while it loads, where gruszka packs and loads before it checks.}
\li{\strong{gruszka repeats} to 0.23 s on Mathlib (10.65 to 10.88 s), 0.04 s on navier-stokes-euler and 0.10 s on cslib over its eight runs, and its times are those of rep-P11 (10.80, 2.65 and 4.04 s), taken five hours earlier in the same night: the conditions of the two series were the same.}
}
}

