\tr{\td{instructions of a cold process, default configuration}\td{70.4 G}\td{121.6 G}\td{172.2 G}\td{15.1 G}\td{155.4 G}\td{420.4 G}\td{498.9 G}\td{1,462.6 G}}
\tr{\td{instructions of a cold process, \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}}\td{227.4 G}\td{375.6 G}\td{476.9 G}\td{104.8 G}\td{748.7 G}\td{2,881.8 G}\td{2,018.6 G}\td{7,804.6 G}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Where the merged executable stands against gruszka, on a quiet host with gruszka in the same windows:} behind on seven of the eight corpora, by 6 to 31 \% for a warm pass (Init 1.15, Std 1.21, cedar 1.06, con-leche 1.28, navier-stokes-euler 1.31, cslib 1.17, Mathlib 1.23 times gruszka's wall clock) and by 12 to 44 \% for one cold process; ahead on reachability, 0.24 and 0.30. It is ahead of nano-lean on all eight (0.64 to 0.92 warm).}
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher} is within 6 \% of gruszka on Init, Std, con-leche, cslib and Mathlib (1.04, 1.03, 1.06, 0.98, 1.02), ahead on cedar and reachability (0.89, 0.18) and 14 \% behind on navier-stokes-euler.}
\li{\strong{These ratios cannot be read against the tables before this report.} Those had the release's executable at 1.00, 0.95 and 1.05 times gruszka's warm time on Init, Std and cedar and at 1.09 on Mathlib, with gruszka measured in windows of its own while the other 12 cores of the host were busy. The busy cores cost gruszka more than RLVM: its rows here are 10 to 32 \% shorter than there (Init 0.71 to 0.53 s, Std 1.28 to 0.87 s, cedar 1.73 to 1.39 s, Mathlib 14.20 to 10.68 s), nano-lean's 6 to 23 \%, while the release's executable is 4 to 14 \% faster on a quiet host than in those tables (its warm passes of section 3 against its rows there: Init 0.676 against 0.71 s, Mathlib 14.57 against 15.46 s). [The profile of the release](rep-P11), section 1.2, had found the same on the three large corpora. The merged executable did not lose ground: it is 3 to 11 \% faster than the release on every corpus under one condition (section 3). What changed is that the comparison is now made where the arena's runner is, on 8 cores with nothing else running, and there gruszka is faster than the earlier tables showed.}
\li{\strong{A cold process against a warm pass}: the executable's cold process is 3 to 11 \% longer than its warm pass on seven corpora (Mathlib 13.53 against 13.15 s) and 22 \% on reachability (0.55 against 0.45 s).}
\li{\strong{The reference engine by name} got faster too, through the loader and the level base: 9 to 22 \% shorter cold processes than its rows before (Mathlib 85.76 to 75.98 s, con-leche 39.15 to 30.54 s; those rows were taken with the other cores busy) and 7 to 10 \% fewer instructions (Mathlib 8,455.5 to 7,804.6 G, Init 247.3 to 227.4 G).}
\li{\strong{Instructions of the default configuration}, one cold process each in the arena-shaped container: 7 to 13 \% below the release's (Mathlib 1,652.9 to 1,462.6 G). Against gruszka's counts with its arena run line, as recorded on this host: 3.1 to 3.6 times on seven corpora, 1.14 times on reachability (before: 3.5 to 4.1 and 1.23).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 The optional build with Oracle GraalVM}

\p{Oracle GraalVM is used by this explicitly optional build only; nothing was downloaded (the toolchain directory had it). The profile was recorded again with \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh profiles --published~} at 10:34 UTC: an instrumented image of the published jars (58 s), the Truffle probe, and Init once in the default configuration (1.5 s instrumented). The record of the profiles in the repository names the jars of the merged build (rlvm-level \code{4db74adbb68d}, rlvm-api \code{e2d2d31509fc}, rlvm-export \code{38c922c4fccd}, rlvm-store \code{a93a52584ee7}, rlvm-load \code{a2a091b291fa}; profile id \code{d9e6c398f79e}, 2.6 MB compressed). The optional executable was then built once from them (39 s; 46.6 MB, sha256 \code{07afab63d8cc}); its build record says "profile state: fresh", and \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} starts its build line with "Oracle GraalVM, profile-guided". The whole command took 103 s.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Its row} (the table above): Mathlib 12.93 s cold and 12.41 s for a warm pass, Init 0.61 and 0.56 s, cslib 4.71 and 4.54 s. Against gruszka's second run its warm pass is at 1.06, 1.14, 1.00, 0.24, 1.20, 1.23, 1.11 and 1.16.}
\li{\strong{Its lead over the default executable has halved}: 0.92 to 0.96 times the default executable's cold wall clock on the eight corpora and 0.92 to 0.98 times its warm pass, where the tables of the release had 0.85 to 0.89 and 0.82 to 0.89. The inline list gives the Community Edition image by name a part of what a profile gives the Oracle image; the profile-guided build takes no list. The sentence of the root README that still says 0.85 to 0.89 is listed in section 6.}
\li{\strong{Not gated.} The optional executable accepted the eight corpora in its 16 benchmark processes; no gate and no arena procedure was run on it.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. What was not measured}

\ul{
\li{\strong{x86-64}, the architecture of the arena's runner: nothing.}
\li{\strong{The guard with the other cores busy}, the condition of the earlier benchmark tables and of most windows of the three single reports.}
\li{\strong{The three changes one by one or in pairs} again: the release and the build with all three are what was compared. Where the sum differs from the product of the single measurements (the JVM launcher, section 3.2) the cause was not separated.}
\li{\strong{One worker}: no timing. The deterministic one-worker runs of section 2.1 were compared by their counters, not by their time. The one-worker rows of the baselines in the benchmark tables are still those of 2026-11-24.}
\li{\strong{The differential engine} of the test kit on the merged build: the changes are cost only, which the counters of section 2.1 show for the eight corpora and the invariance suite for the schedules.}
\li{\strong{The invariance suite} on the mutants of the corpora \code{mdata} and \code{prelude}, on the four large exports, and with the reference engine as primary engine. The three single reports ran the large exports and the reference engine on their candidates.}
\li{\strong{gruszka and nano-lean inside the guard's windows} of the five smaller corpora: their rows there are the two runs of the benchmark window of section 4, taken 5 to 10 minutes after those windows. nano-lean was in no guard window.}
\li{\strong{The release-layout executable} was run through the arena's procedure and not timed in the guard; it is a second build of the same jars.}
\li{\strong{The optional Oracle build}: no gate, no arena procedure, no guard; its rows are single processes.}
\li{\strong{The fast / slow splits and the pooled session arrays}, the two changes that stay out: neither was built or measured here. The reasons are the measurements of [rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A), section 5.3, and of [the insight on the pooled arrays](ins-JJ7I).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. What this work did not change, and what is left for others}

\p{No source and no script was changed. Tracked files that changed: the trees of this journal, the record and the two files of the profiles of the optional build (through the retraining command), and in the root README the generated table with the sentence of its conditions ("idle" for "busy", one date) and the sentence on the arena's complete test set in the paragraph of the gates. Stale after this wave and not touched, because they are outside what this work was allowed to edit:}

\ul{
\li{The root README, section "Get it": the optional build "needs 0.85 to 0.89 times the default executable's wall clock on the eight corpora". Measured now: 0.92 to 0.96 (section 4.1).}
\li{\src{rlvm-driver/README.md}, the paragraph on the hand-off interval: "each worker context builds a new level overlay at its first reset after a new snapshot (the level and list tables are copied), about 110 MB of garbage and 45 ms of worker CPU per publication". Since the shared level base nothing is copied by a worker; [the driver](design-driver) has the new state.}
\li{\src{rlvm-load/README.md}, "Keeping up with the frontier costs a worker one level view (a copy of the level arrays ...)": a measurement of the layout before the shared base.}
\li{\src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}, the header comment: it cites the arena's procedure on the frozen jar set of the release ([rep-AR2](rep-AR2)); the same procedure on the merged build is section 2.3 of this report, with 1,462.3 G instructions on Mathlib.}
\li{No session tree was written for the wave; the trees of this work are listed in [the decision](dec-JT59) and below.}
}

\p{Trees of this work. New: this report, [the decision](dec-JT59), the changelog entries [log-E7BA](log-E7BA), [log-ZMAE](log-ZMAE) and [log-R7NE](log-R7NE). Regenerated: [the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks). Revised: [universe levels](design-universe-levels), [the driver](design-driver), [the export loader](design-export-loader), [the launchers](design-launchers), [packaging](design-packaging), [how to read the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks-notes), the index, and a paragraph each in [what the release leaves open](prob-HC9J) and [the loader as the floor](prob-I7M9).}
}
